WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.590 --> 00:00:06.170
We predicted this would happen, but now it's
time to really get our hands sort of dirty

00:00:06.170 --> 00:00:08.740
because the treasury led by Trump flunky.

00:00:08.740 --> 00:00:14.160
Steven Minutian is officially refusing to
turn over Donald Trump's tax returns, which

00:00:14.160 --> 00:00:20.760
were requested legally, uh, by Congressman
Richard Neil on the basis of a law from the

00:00:20.760 --> 00:00:26.270
1920s, uh, in his role as chairman of the
house ways and means committee.

00:00:26.270 --> 00:00:31.269
The reason why minutia is refusing to turn
over Trump's tax returns is exactly the reason

00:00:31.269 --> 00:00:36.660
that we predicted, which is that the requests
from committee chairman Richard Neal quotes,

00:00:36.660 --> 00:00:45.050
lacks a legitimate legislative purpose, which
minutian claims is required by the supreme

00:00:45.050 --> 00:00:49.120
court in over to, in order to turn over tax
returns.

00:00:49.120 --> 00:00:52.309
Unfortunately, it doesn't appear that that's
actually the case.

00:00:52.309 --> 00:00:55.320
So I'm going to explain this all to you piece
by piece.

00:00:55.320 --> 00:01:01.860
Uh, and what you have to remember is that
Stephen Minutian was advised by Trump's justice

00:01:01.860 --> 00:01:06.459
department in terms of how to not provide
these tax returns.

00:01:06.459 --> 00:01:11.889
The Trump justice department is led of course,
by none other than Attorney General William

00:01:11.889 --> 00:01:18.740
Barr, a professional at covering up the misdeeds
of presidents of the United States.

00:01:18.740 --> 00:01:20.850
So this is a loss.

00:01:20.850 --> 00:01:25.990
If you were expecting that today, we would
get Donald Trump's tax returns, but it's completely

00:01:25.990 --> 00:01:31.210
expected and it's now going to set off a legal
battle that could actually be positive in

00:01:31.210 --> 00:01:34.340
a way, in the sense that it's going to put
Donald Trump's tax returns.

00:01:34.340 --> 00:01:38.889
And more importantly, the fact that we haven't
seen them back in the news, which strategically

00:01:38.889 --> 00:01:43.930
is good for Democrats because most Americans
want to see Donald Trump's taxes.

00:01:43.930 --> 00:01:45.450
So that's the strategy.

00:01:45.450 --> 00:01:47.979
Let's first go through the legality of this.

00:01:47.979 --> 00:01:51.419
There's a law that's very clear.

00:01:51.419 --> 00:01:52.419
Okay.

00:01:52.419 --> 00:01:55.880
The law dates back to the 1920s, I think it's
24, but I'm going from memory.

00:01:55.880 --> 00:02:01.439
And the law says that if you're a member of
the House and Senate, a house or Senate who

00:02:01.439 --> 00:02:05.989
is on a tax writing committee and there are
a number of committees that have tax writing

00:02:05.989 --> 00:02:12.300
privileges, you were able to legally request
the tax returns of any taxpayer and that the

00:02:12.300 --> 00:02:15.110
IRS quote shall furnish them.

00:02:15.110 --> 00:02:16.110
Period.

00:02:16.110 --> 00:02:17.379
There are no caveats there.

00:02:17.379 --> 00:02:18.819
There are no ifs, ands and buts.

00:02:18.819 --> 00:02:22.920
You can request them if you're on one of those
committees and the IRS shall furnish them.

00:02:22.920 --> 00:02:28.069
It doesn't allow for getting help from the
Justice Department to try to block the request.

00:02:28.069 --> 00:02:32.489
Something which a president can try, but most
citizens would not be able to try to get the

00:02:32.489 --> 00:02:36.090
Justice Department to help them block the
release of their tax returns.

00:02:36.090 --> 00:02:41.379
But the one question about all of this was,
is there any precedent which says that if

00:02:41.379 --> 00:02:46.439
you want to request the tax return from an
individual taxpayer on this basis using this

00:02:46.439 --> 00:02:51.640
law, that you need to justify a legislative
purpose for the request.

00:02:51.640 --> 00:02:58.499
If there is Richard Neil says the committee
is looking into whether mandatory audits of

00:02:58.499 --> 00:03:01.060
sitting presidents are good policy.

00:03:01.060 --> 00:03:07.400
So he's come up with a reason, a justification,
a legislative justification if indeed one

00:03:07.400 --> 00:03:08.400
is needed.

00:03:08.400 --> 00:03:13.370
Now, the truth is that claiming you need a
reason is pretty dubious.

00:03:13.370 --> 00:03:17.540
And the reason that we know that's the case
is by looking at the law.

00:03:17.540 --> 00:03:24.459
If you look at 26 us code subsection 61 oh
three, it doesn't say anything about a legislative

00:03:24.459 --> 00:03:25.459
purpose.

00:03:25.459 --> 00:03:29.260
It says just quote upon written request from
the chairman of the committee on House on

00:03:29.260 --> 00:03:33.810
ways and means of the House of Representatives,
the chairman of the Committee on finance of

00:03:33.810 --> 00:03:39.499
the Senate or the chairman of the joint committee
on taxation, the secretary of the treasury.

00:03:39.499 --> 00:03:45.270
That means shelf furnished such committee
with any return or return information specified

00:03:45.270 --> 00:03:51.230
in such request, except that any return or
return information which can be associated

00:03:51.230 --> 00:03:57.629
with or otherwise identified directly or indirectly
a particular taxpayer shall be furnished to

00:03:57.629 --> 00:04:03.930
such committee only when sitting in closed
executive session unless such taxpayer otherwise

00:04:03.930 --> 00:04:06.260
consents in writing to such disclosure.

00:04:06.260 --> 00:04:12.560
So what it says is that it has to be submitted,
uh, um, uh, privately, right in executive

00:04:12.560 --> 00:04:17.540
session, unless the taxpayer says it's okay
for it to be made more widely available.

00:04:17.540 --> 00:04:21.329
It says nothing about legislative purpose.

00:04:21.329 --> 00:04:26.740
Minutian has come up with a viable explanation,
but it's nowhere in the law.

00:04:26.740 --> 00:04:32.810
It says that the secretary of the Treasury
shall furnish, not secretary of the Treasury

00:04:32.810 --> 00:04:38.669
will analyze whether they believe there is
a legislative purpose and devaluate whether

00:04:38.669 --> 00:04:42.550
in their opinion it is a legitimate legislative
purpose.

00:04:42.550 --> 00:04:46.030
This is arguably another act of obstruction.

00:04:46.030 --> 00:04:53.509
If Trump directed the justice department to
help the treasury not follow the law, it is

00:04:53.509 --> 00:04:58.100
another instance and they're already 14 others,
which we'll talk about later.

00:04:58.100 --> 00:05:02.479
It is another instance of obstructing the
process of justice.

00:05:02.479 --> 00:05:08.820
It's another check box, so to speak, in the
list of things Nixon resigned over which Donald

00:05:08.820 --> 00:05:10.169
Trump has also done.

00:05:10.169 --> 00:05:12.080
So that's the legal side of it.

00:05:12.080 --> 00:05:14.580
Then there's the strategy piece.

00:05:14.580 --> 00:05:17.759
Seeing Trump's tax returns is very popular
with Americans.

00:05:17.759 --> 00:05:19.949
It goes beyond just Democrats.

00:05:19.949 --> 00:05:24.169
So simply keeping this in the news is probably
a good strategy, particularly as a reminder,

00:05:24.169 --> 00:05:29.770
as 2020 sort of heats up that Donald Trump
still hasn't released his taxes and is actively

00:05:29.770 --> 00:05:34.990
using his Justice Department and the Treasury
to keep those tax returns secrets.

00:05:34.990 --> 00:05:40.780
So even if this fails to get Trump's tax returns
over to the house ways and means committee,

00:05:40.780 --> 00:05:45.180
which it very well may because this is an
administration that just ignores subpoenas,

00:05:45.180 --> 00:05:50.129
ignores requests and they don't seem to be
suffering any consequences for that.

00:05:50.129 --> 00:05:55.849
Uh, even if it doesn't achieve that, it still
is likely a useful thing for Democrats in

00:05:55.849 --> 00:06:00.630
2020 just as a reminder that we've still not
seeing those tax returns and Donald Trump

00:06:00.630 --> 00:06:04.790
is going even more out of his way then he
has at any previous point to prevent those

00:06:04.790 --> 00:06:06.750
tax returns from going public.

00:06:06.750 --> 00:06:08.090
Last caveat.

00:06:08.090 --> 00:06:12.849
If you think that the tax returns, if we saw
them, would expose criminality by Trump?

00:06:12.849 --> 00:06:13.849
I don't think so.

